WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.260
ELIAS PINTO: Hello, and
welcome to Google Cloud On Air.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:08.830
These are the live
webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:12.010
Today, we are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:14.000
My name is Elias Pinto.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:15.235
And with me, I have--

00:00:15.235 --> 00:00:16.680
SARA ROBINSON:
I'm Sara Robinson.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.710
ELIAS PINTO: We're
based out of New York.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:22.580
And today, we will be
talking about AutoML Vision.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.900
To let you know, you can
ask questions any time

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.570
on the platform, and we
have Googlers on standby

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:29.990
to answer them.

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:31.280
So let's get started.

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:32.920
So here is Sara.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.540
SARA ROBINSON: Thanks, Elias.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.030
So today, we're going to
talk about AutoML Vision,

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:41.332
making custom image analysis
possible for every business.

00:00:41.332 --> 00:00:42.790
Before we get into
that, let's talk

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:44.530
about what machine learning is.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.360
So at a high level,
machine learning

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.670
involves teaching computers
to recognize patterns

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.890
in the same way
that our brains do.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.740
Over time, as machine learning
models are given more examples

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.940
and experience, they're able
to improve and generalize

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.566
on the input that they
haven't seen before.

00:01:00.566 --> 00:01:01.940
So does anybody
remember how they

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.549
learned their first language?

00:01:03.549 --> 00:01:05.090
Your parents probably
didn't give you

00:01:05.090 --> 00:01:07.460
a dictionary and a bunch of
grammar books to memorize.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:08.690
That'd be kind of weird.

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:10.970
Instead, you learned over
time by being exposed

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.420
to many different examples.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.600
So let's say you were
having pasta for dinner.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:15.920
You saw it on your plate.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.690
You heard your
parents identify it.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.790
Maybe you identified it
incorrectly a few times

00:01:19.790 --> 00:01:21.080
and were corrected.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.870
Over time, this repetition
strengthens certain pathways

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:24.950
in your brain.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.740
And this is roughly how
machine learning works too.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.990
So at a high level,
machine learning

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.960
is loosely based on how
the human brain learns.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.790
Instead of biological
neurons, we

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:36.770
have mathematical
neurons that mimic

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.630
these neurons in our brain.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.940
With machine learning,
we can solve problems

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.959
without exactly knowing
what the solution might be.

00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:45.500
And finally, machine
learning enables

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.870
systems that improve
over time as they're

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.310
given more and more data.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:53.800
So before I start solving
a machine learning problem

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:55.383
and picking the tool
I'm going to use,

00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:58.690
I like to think about the type
of ML problem that I'm solving.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.550
And on Google Cloud
Platform, we have a spectrum

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.055
of machine learning offerings.

00:02:04.055 --> 00:02:05.430
So on the left-hand
side, we have

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.195
products that are targeted more
towards application developers.

00:02:08.195 --> 00:02:09.570
If you're new to
machine learning

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:11.890
and don't have any machine
learning experience,

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:13.632
we offer five pre-trained APIs.

00:02:13.632 --> 00:02:15.840
These give you access to
pre-trained machine learning

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:16.620
models.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:18.180
All you need to
do to access them

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.640
is make one REST API request.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.344
On the right side
of the spectrum,

00:02:22.344 --> 00:02:24.510
we have products that are
targeted more towards data

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:26.635
scientists and those with
a little bit more machine

00:02:26.635 --> 00:02:27.477
learning experience.

00:02:27.477 --> 00:02:30.060
And for this, we have TensorFlow
for building your own machine

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.880
learning models and ML
Engine as our managed

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.320
platform for TensorFlow.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.080
So going a little
bit deeper into this,

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:39.640
if you've got a
common classification

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.530
task that someone else
has solved before,

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:42.860
you don't need to
start from scratch.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:44.890
You don't need to build a model
from scratch, trained only

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:45.970
on your own data.

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:48.040
You can utilize existing
pre-trained models

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.324
that are out there.

00:02:49.324 --> 00:02:50.740
But if you've got
a problem that's

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:52.390
very specific to
the type of data

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:54.409
that you're dealing
with at your company,

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:56.950
it's probably going to be a more
custom task that you'll need

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.720
a more customized solution for.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.119
So let's take image
classification as an example.

00:03:02.119 --> 00:03:03.910
Let's say you want to
identify that there's

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:04.960
a cat in this image.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.229
And you've probably heard
this example before.

00:03:07.229 --> 00:03:08.770
This has been done
many times before.

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:10.360
There's lots of
models that exist out

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.280
there that have been trained
on hundreds of thousands

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.830
of images that can tell you
if there is a cat in the image

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:15.670
or not.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.919
So there's no need to start
from scratch, although you

00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:19.679
can if you'd like,
but it's fastest

00:03:19.679 --> 00:03:21.970
to utilize a pre-trained
model that's already out there

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.290
to help you with this.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.810
But let's say, for example,
that this is your cat.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:26.800
Its name is Chloe.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.840
And you want to
identify Chloe apart

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.590
from other cats in
your image data set.

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:33.970
So this is a more custom task.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:37.600
You're going to need to train
a model on your own data

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.300
using label data so that
it can identify Chloe apart

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.374
from other cats and animals
in your image library.

00:03:45.374 --> 00:03:47.290
So let's start with the
machine learning APIs.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.610
We offer five
different APIs that

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:51.350
give you access to
a pre-trained model

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.632
to accomplish common
machine learning tasks.

00:03:53.632 --> 00:03:55.340
So you don't really
need to know anything

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.424
about how these models work
under the hood to get started.

00:03:58.424 --> 00:04:00.590
You just pass in your image
data for the Vision API,

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:01.089
for example.

00:04:01.089 --> 00:04:02.840
You get back the classification.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.090
The Video API does what
the Vision API does

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:06.410
for images, but for videos.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:08.170
It lets you analyze videos.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:12.895
The Speech API lets you perform
speech-to-text transcription.

00:04:12.895 --> 00:04:14.270
With the Natural
Language API, we

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.250
can further analyze that text.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.040
And with the translation
API, we can translate text

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:21.380
into over 100
different languages.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.390
So these are our
pre-trained APIs

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:25.370
targeted towards
application developers who

00:04:25.370 --> 00:04:26.960
want to integrate
machine learning

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:29.870
into their applications.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:31.732
On the other side
of the spectrum,

00:04:31.732 --> 00:04:33.440
we have tools to help
you build and train

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.430
your models from scratch.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.430
So from the beginning,
the Google Brain team

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:38.990
wanted everyone in
the industry to be

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.600
able to benefit from all of
the machine learning projects

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:42.930
that they were working on.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:45.860
So they made TensorFlow, an
open source project on GitHub.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:47.820
And the uptake has
been phenomenal.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.720
TensorFlow is the most popular
machine learning project

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:51.770
on GitHub.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:56.210
Last time I checked, I think it
had over 90,000 GitHub stars.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:59.130
It also just crossed
over a million downloads.

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.820
It's being used
all over the world.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:02.880
And because TensorFlow
is open source,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:07.252
you can train and serve your
TensorFlow models anywhere.

00:05:07.252 --> 00:05:09.460
Because we're focused on
Google Cloud Platform today,

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:10.918
I'm going to talk
about our managed

00:05:10.918 --> 00:05:12.040
platform for TensorFlow.

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:14.860
This is called Cloud
Machine Learning Engine.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:17.380
And it lets you take advantage
of distributed training.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.230
You can distribute your
training job across machines,

00:05:20.230 --> 00:05:23.830
using GPUs or TPUs to
accelerate your machine learning

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:25.060
workloads.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.800
Once your model's
trained, you can then

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.830
choose to deploy it to Machine
Learning Engine for serving.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:31.834
So let's say your ML
model becomes a huge hit.

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:34.000
You're getting thousands
and thousands of prediction

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.332
requests every second.

00:05:35.332 --> 00:05:36.790
You're going to
need a scalable way

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:38.850
to handle all those requests.

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:41.350
And you can use Machine Learning
Engine to serve your model.

00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:43.832
The great thing about ML
Engine is there's no lock in.

00:05:43.832 --> 00:05:45.790
You can choose to train
your model on ML Engine

00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:49.060
and then download it, export
it, and serve it somewhere else.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:50.835
Optionally, you
can train the model

00:05:50.835 --> 00:05:52.960
wherever you'd like and
decide you want to serve it

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:56.224
on Machine Learning Engine.

00:05:56.224 --> 00:05:57.890
Another thing I like
to think about when

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.640
I'm diving into a
machine learning problem

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:02.586
is the resources that I
need to solve that problem.

00:06:02.586 --> 00:06:03.710
I've listed just four here.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:05.870
There's probably others
that I haven't thought of.

00:06:05.870 --> 00:06:07.470
But let's go over
what these are.

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:08.780
So first is training data.

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:10.280
How much training
data are you going

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.940
to need to get an accurate model
that's going to get an accurate

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:15.650
prediction on your data?

00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:18.710
How much model code
are you going to write?

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.570
How much training and
serving infrastructure

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:21.740
are you going to
have to provision?

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.480
And then, finally,
overall, how much time

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:25.670
is this whole process
going to take you?

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:27.920
So let's look at how this
applies to the products

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.200
that I've talked about so far.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.470
So if you look at machine
learning as an API,

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:34.970
the great thing
about these APIs is

00:06:34.970 --> 00:06:37.053
that you don't need any
training data to use them.

00:06:37.053 --> 00:06:38.580
The model has
already been trained.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:39.782
They're pre-trained models.

00:06:39.782 --> 00:06:41.990
So if I wanted to, I could
just generate a prediction

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:43.227
on a single image.

00:06:43.227 --> 00:06:44.435
I pass that image to the API.

00:06:44.435 --> 00:06:46.010
I get a prediction back.

00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:49.519
But it didn't require any
of my own training data.

00:06:49.519 --> 00:06:51.560
I also don't need to write
any of the model code.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.560
Again, that's all handled
for you by the API.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.497
An API is available on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:06:56.497 --> 00:06:58.580
So I don't need to worry
about training or serving

00:06:58.580 --> 00:06:59.780
infrastructure.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:01.760
All it's going to take
is a little bit of time

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.770
to write my request to
the API and figure out

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:06.250
how I want to parse the
response that I get back.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:08.000
You can get up and
running with these APIs

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.720
in probably less than a day.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.280
Now, we'll talk
about two approaches

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:14.610
to building a custom model.

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:16.459
The first is transfer
learning, which

00:07:16.459 --> 00:07:18.500
lets you take advantage
of a model that's already

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.777
been trained to do a similar
task to what you'd like to do.

00:07:21.777 --> 00:07:24.110
So let's say you want identify
where certain objects are

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:25.036
in an image.

00:07:25.036 --> 00:07:27.410
There's lots of models that
have been trained to do this.

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:30.440
So you can utilize the weights
of these trained models

00:07:30.440 --> 00:07:32.600
and then just modify the
last couple of layers,

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:34.610
based on your own training data.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:37.100
So with this, you will need
some of your own training data

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:39.590
because the predictions
are going to be

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:41.420
specific to your data set.

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.490
You will need to write
some of the model code,

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:45.392
and you may need to
think about provisioning

00:07:45.392 --> 00:07:46.850
training or serving
infrastructure.

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:49.850
Or you may choose to use a
managed service for this.

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:52.210
And this will obviously
take a little bit more time

00:07:52.210 --> 00:07:55.070
than the pre-trained APIs.

00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:57.470
And then finally, if you've
got a model built entirely

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:59.324
from scratch, trained
only on your data,

00:07:59.324 --> 00:08:01.490
you're going to need a lot
of training data for this

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:03.214
to work successfully.

00:08:03.214 --> 00:08:05.630
You're also going to need to
write a lot of the model code

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.727
yourself, think
about where you're

00:08:07.727 --> 00:08:09.185
going to run your
training, and how

00:08:09.185 --> 00:08:11.184
you're going to serve
your model and production.

00:08:11.184 --> 00:08:13.850
And obviously, this one
will take even more time

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:17.620
than with transfer learning.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:20.330
So focusing specifically
on image analysis,

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:22.120
let's take a look at
the existing tools

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:25.310
for image analysis on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.250
For that, we have
the Cloud Vision API,

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:31.150
which lets you perform image
analysis with a single REST API

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.830
request.

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:37.280
These are all of the features
that the Vision API provides.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:40.350
So at its core, the Vision
API offers label detection,

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:42.799
which will tell you what
is this a picture of.

00:08:42.799 --> 00:08:45.530
So for this image, it might
return elephant, animal,

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:46.970
et cetera.

00:08:46.970 --> 00:08:49.580
Then we have web detection,
which goes a step further.

00:08:49.580 --> 00:08:52.790
And it will look across the
internet for similar images.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:54.800
And then based on the
content of those pages,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.420
it will provide
additional details

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:58.770
on what's in your image.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:02.820
Then we have OCR or Optical
Character Recognition.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.469
OCR is able to extract
text from images.

00:09:05.469 --> 00:09:07.635
So if any of you have ever
used the Google Translate

00:09:07.635 --> 00:09:10.110
app before, and taken
a picture of a sign,

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:12.300
and then seen the
translation in real time,

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:14.520
you can use the Vision
API's OCR method

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.140
to implement similar
functionality

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.540
into your own applications.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:21.655
Logo detection will identify
common logos in an image.

00:09:21.655 --> 00:09:24.820
Landmark detection we'll
find landmarks in an image.

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:27.510
Crop hints can help
you crop your photo

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:30.040
to focus on specific
subjects in the image.

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.820
And then finally, we have
explicit content detection.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:34.290
This is pretty self-explanatory.

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:37.440
It will tell you is your
image appropriate or not.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.240
This one is pretty
useful across the board

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:42.570
for any site or app that
has user-generated content.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.430
Instead of having
someone manually review

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.350
all of those images
that come in,

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:47.917
you can send it
to the Vision API,

00:09:47.917 --> 00:09:49.500
and then maybe you
only need to review

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.745
a subset of those images.

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:55.830
As I mentioned, the
Vision API is a REST API.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:57.960
So you can call it in
any language you'd like.

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.690
The example code I have
here is in Node.js.

00:10:00.690 --> 00:10:02.730
And on Google Cloud
Platform, we have a number

00:10:02.730 --> 00:10:05.520
of client libraries in
your favorite language

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:08.310
that make it easy to
call all these APIs.

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:12.390
So I'm using the Google Cloud
node module for the Vision API.

00:10:12.390 --> 00:10:15.510
Here I tell it the types
of detection I want to run.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.900
I call detect on
that Vision object.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:20.940
I can either pass it
a local image URL,

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:24.370
or I can pass it the URL of
a file stored in Google Cloud

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:25.530
Storage.

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:28.890
I can also just pass it the raw
image content, Base64 encoded.

00:10:28.890 --> 00:10:31.440
So I pass it that image,
the types of detection I

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:32.280
want to run.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:34.500
And then I get a response
back with the detections.

00:10:34.500 --> 00:10:36.360
And I can analyze
that response however

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:40.490
I'd like to use that
data in my application.

00:10:40.490 --> 00:10:42.649
So I don't like to get too
far into a presentation

00:10:42.649 --> 00:10:43.815
without showing a live demo.

00:10:43.815 --> 00:10:45.660
So I'm going to
bring Elias on here

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:49.402
to show us a demo of Vision API.

00:10:49.402 --> 00:10:50.610
ELIAS PINTO: Thank you, Sara.

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:54.450
So here we have a chance to
actually see how powerful

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.520
the Cloud Vision API really is.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:59.100
And to build on
what Sara just said,

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:02.600
It gives the ability to
understand what's in an image.

00:11:02.600 --> 00:11:07.380
And Google is very
uniquely positioned

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:09.900
to provide you with powerful
machine learning models.

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:12.120
And let me show
you how easy it is.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:15.720
This is a publicly available
demo that you can do.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:18.660
And we're going to go
and actually pull up

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.520
some images here.

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:24.990
So Sara was kind enough to
share some of her recent holiday

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:29.460
pictures, and we pulled up a
picture she took in Poland.

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:35.160
And right away, what you see is
that the machine or the model

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:38.190
immediately recognized that
this was taken in Krakow.

00:11:38.190 --> 00:11:41.000
Or if you're Polish, I think
you pronounce it "Krakoff."

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:44.700
And it not only
recognizes that landmark,

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:48.360
but it's able to find out
that it's also the main market

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.140
square.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:51.170
And because of
Google Maps, we were

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:54.630
able to pull exactly
the lat/long of where

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:56.100
that picture was taken.

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:59.760
Let's look a little bit
more into the other labels

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:02.040
that we have, the other
entities that we recognize.

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:04.170
So we recognize the face.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:06.940
Here we see the human
face is picked up.

00:12:06.940 --> 00:12:09.750
But also, please note,
the exact location

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:14.760
of things, like eyes, nose, and
mouth, is correctly identified.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:18.640
We're able to also determine
sentiment analysis.

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:22.080
So Sara here looks very
joyful and even surprised

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:23.440
at that picture.

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:26.310
And as you can see
there, very likely,

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:30.137
those are the emotions she is
displaying on that picture.

00:12:30.137 --> 00:12:31.470
It must have been a fun holiday.

00:12:31.470 --> 00:12:32.490
SARA ROBINSON: It was.

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:34.320
ELIAS PINTO: So
we can quickly see

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:37.110
there is a very
long list of labels

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:41.040
that we automatically
assign to that picture.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:47.380
And we're able to see that
under the web entities,

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.620
we were able to
use the knowledge

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:52.470
of the web about that
picture and actually pull it.

00:12:52.470 --> 00:12:54.900
And it does an image
search and compares

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:57.500
that with the information
available on the web.

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:01.530
I'll briefly mentioned here
that we also have Safe Search.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.320
We can very quickly identify
that, yes, this is just

00:13:04.320 --> 00:13:08.670
a plain holiday picture, and
it's safe to show at any age.

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:11.587
The last thing I want to
show is the JSON output.

00:13:11.587 --> 00:13:13.170
It's a very long
output, but I'll just

00:13:13.170 --> 00:13:16.620
highlight some of the
types and positions

00:13:16.620 --> 00:13:17.700
of the different labels.

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:20.820
So you can see the left eye,
the right eye, and so on and so

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:24.920
forth for each one of the labels
that it identified, it pulls.

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:28.650
It gives you exactly where
it is located on the image.

00:13:28.650 --> 00:13:33.910
Let's pull up another image,
and this one is a little busier.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.900
Sara, again, was very lucky
to have gone to "Hamilton--"

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:39.364
the show very recently--
so she shared a picture.

00:13:39.364 --> 00:13:41.280
As you can see, this is
a much busier picture.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:43.620
There's a lot more going
on, a lot more different

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:49.230
labels and other
elements in this picture.

00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:52.560
So under faces, right away, we
picked her face here, again,

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.660
with a sentiment analysis.

00:13:55.660 --> 00:13:57.600
Now here's the interesting bit.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.630
We can show things
like on the web,

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:04.320
it recognized correctly this
was the Richard Rodgers Theatre.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:07.120
And under text, I don't
know if you noticed,

00:14:07.120 --> 00:14:09.840
but the word "playbill" is
actually in the bottom there.

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:13.650
This highlights the intelligence
around OCR that we have.

00:14:13.650 --> 00:14:16.080
Even if there's multiple
words in the picture,

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:17.940
we're able to
extract that to you.

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:22.050
If it's a piece of text
under the document,

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:27.240
say it's a menu, or
a form, or a resume,

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:30.930
we're able to pick
different blocks of text

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:34.550
and display them under
pages and blocks.

00:14:34.550 --> 00:14:37.050
So these are some of the things
that we wanted to highlight.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:40.030
The good thing is that this
is a publicly available under

00:14:40.030 --> 00:14:42.630
a cloud.google.com/vision.

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:46.690
And you may test this
on your own today.

00:14:46.690 --> 00:14:48.120
A quick reminder, if I may.

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:50.280
If you want to ask
questions, we ask you

00:14:50.280 --> 00:14:54.090
that you put those questions
throughout our presentation,

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:57.930
so we're able to bubble them up
towards the end of our session.

00:14:57.930 --> 00:14:58.680
Back to Sara.

00:14:58.680 --> 00:15:00.013
SARA ROBINSON: Thank you, Elias.

00:15:00.013 --> 00:15:00.616
Great demo.

00:15:00.616 --> 00:15:03.730
So back to the slides.

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:05.570
I want to revisit the
ML spectrum that I

00:15:05.570 --> 00:15:07.095
covered in the beginning.

00:15:07.095 --> 00:15:08.720
So remember that on
the left-hand side,

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:11.480
we have products targeted
towards app developers, machine

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:12.390
learning APIs.

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:13.967
These are pre-trained APIs.

00:15:13.967 --> 00:15:15.800
You can't customize
them at all, but they're

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:18.054
very easy to get up
and running with.

00:15:18.054 --> 00:15:19.470
On the other side
of the spectrum,

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:22.460
we have products targeted
towards data scientists

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:24.170
and machine learning
practitioners.

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:25.940
And these allow you a
lot more flexibility

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:29.160
in building a custom model
trained on your own data set.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:32.120
So this is what the spectrum
looked like until just a couple

00:15:32.120 --> 00:15:33.180
of months ago.

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:36.080
I'm super excited
about this new product

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:39.020
that we announced in
January called AutoML.

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:41.715
The first product under
AutoML is AutoML Vision.

00:15:41.715 --> 00:15:43.340
And it's currently
in alpha so you need

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:44.600
to be whitelisted to use it.

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:47.000
But what AutoML does
is, you can see,

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:49.650
it clearly fills a
gap in the spectrum.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:52.850
What it does is AutoML Vision
lets you customize the Vision

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:55.280
API to your own data set.

00:15:55.280 --> 00:15:58.640
So let's say the Vision API
works pretty well for you,

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:00.830
but you've got some
very specific images

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:03.430
that you wouldn't expect it
to recognize what they are,

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:06.090
but you want to generate
labels, based on your company's

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:07.990
very specific data set.

00:16:07.990 --> 00:16:11.900
Now you can do that with AutoML.

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:13.880
So this is what AutoML
Vision provides.

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:15.920
It's essentially a
UI that helps you

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:19.940
with every step of building your
model, from importing the data,

00:16:19.940 --> 00:16:23.090
labeling it, training a
model, evaluating how well it

00:16:23.090 --> 00:16:26.060
performed, and then
finally, making predictions

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:29.730
on that model, using data
that it hasn't seen before.

00:16:29.730 --> 00:16:31.820
So the best way to
show you AutoML Vision

00:16:31.820 --> 00:16:34.970
is to jump right into a demo.

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:38.390
So for this demo, let's say
that I am a meteorologist.

00:16:38.390 --> 00:16:41.630
Maybe I work at a
weather company,

00:16:41.630 --> 00:16:44.420
and I want to predict
weather trends and flight

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:47.160
plans from images of clouds.

00:16:47.160 --> 00:16:49.040
So this begs the
question, can we

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:51.590
use the cloud to analyze clouds?

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:54.800
The answer, as you
probably guessed, is yes.

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:56.717
So as I was preparing
this demo, I

00:16:56.717 --> 00:16:58.550
didn't know much about
actual clouds before.

00:16:58.550 --> 00:16:59.250
So I learned a lot.

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:01.749
And I learned that there are
many different types of clouds,

00:17:01.749 --> 00:17:04.310
as you can see
here, and they all

00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:07.274
indicate different kinds
of weather patterns.

00:17:07.274 --> 00:17:09.690
So my first thought was to try
this out with a Vision API.

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:12.619
I took a bunch of these
different cloud images

00:17:12.619 --> 00:17:15.500
and sent them to the Vision
API to see what I got back.

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:18.200
Now for us, it's easy to
see, as humans, that these

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:20.810
are completely different
types of clouds,

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:24.459
but the Vision API was trained
on a huge variety of labels

00:17:24.459 --> 00:17:26.000
to recognize high-level
things, so it

00:17:26.000 --> 00:17:28.369
knows that there are
clouds in these images.

00:17:28.369 --> 00:17:31.370
But even though these images are
very different types of clouds,

00:17:31.370 --> 00:17:35.070
we get similar labels
back from the Vision API.

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:38.510
So this is where AutoML
Vision comes to the rescue.

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:40.880
So let's see a demo
of AutoML Vision.

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:42.780
Go back to the demo.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:47.570
And what we have here is
the UI for AutoML Vision.

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:50.480
So as you can see, it
takes us through each step

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:53.930
of building our model, from
importing data, label, train,

00:17:53.930 --> 00:17:56.220
evaluate, and predict.

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:59.054
So the first step here
is to import our data,

00:17:59.054 --> 00:18:00.720
and there's a couple
of ways to do this.

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:03.717
We can put all of our images
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:18:03.717 --> 00:18:06.050
and then we need to create a
CSV, where the first column

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:08.012
will be the URL of that image.

00:18:08.012 --> 00:18:09.470
The second column
will be the label

00:18:09.470 --> 00:18:11.490
associated with that image.

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:13.190
Now we can also
build models that

00:18:13.190 --> 00:18:15.890
have multiple labels for
images for one image.

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:17.930
Then you just add extra
columns to the CSV

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:19.650
for that particular image.

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:22.490
So in this case, I've already
uploaded all of my data

00:18:22.490 --> 00:18:23.810
and trained the model.

00:18:23.810 --> 00:18:27.600
So let's see what happens
here in the labeling step.

00:18:27.600 --> 00:18:30.890
So here is where I can
review my image labels.

00:18:30.890 --> 00:18:33.050
So I can see what I have
assigned each image.

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:34.550
This data set, I've
already labeled.

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:36.980
So this, for example,
is a cumulus cloud.

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:38.880
We've got a cirrus cloud here.

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:40.670
Let's say I labeled
this one incorrectly.

00:18:40.670 --> 00:18:44.820
I can click on it and
switch out the label here.

00:18:44.820 --> 00:18:47.780
But let's say, for example,
that I did not have time

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:50.300
to label my data set, or
I had a giant data set,

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:52.780
and there was no way I would
be able to label it all.

00:18:52.780 --> 00:18:56.090
AutoML Vision provides an
in-house human labeling

00:18:56.090 --> 00:18:57.110
service.

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:00.230
So the way this works is you
provide a set of instructions

00:19:00.230 --> 00:19:02.740
and some exemplar
images for each label

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:04.040
to these human labelers.

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:05.750
And in just a couple
of days, you'll

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:09.410
get back a labeled
image data set.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:11.630
For this case, I already
had a label data set,

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:14.854
and just to look at a breakdown
of how many images of each type

00:19:14.854 --> 00:19:17.270
of cloud I have-- here I'm
looking at five different types

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:18.450
of clouds.

00:19:18.450 --> 00:19:22.550
And we can see how many
images I have for each label.

00:19:22.550 --> 00:19:24.410
You only need 10
images per label

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:26.010
to start training a model.

00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:28.285
But AutoML recommends
at least 100

00:19:28.285 --> 00:19:29.960
for high-quality predictions.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:32.300
And you'll obviously need
more, depending on what

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:35.670
you're trying to predict.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:37.850
So the next step is
training your model.

00:19:37.850 --> 00:19:39.325
And the great thing
about AutoML is

00:19:39.325 --> 00:19:41.450
that you don't need to
write any of the model code.

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:45.170
That's handled by AutoML
for you automatically.

00:19:45.170 --> 00:19:48.235
All we need to do is literally
just press this Train button,

00:19:48.235 --> 00:19:49.610
and we can choose
whether we want

00:19:49.610 --> 00:19:53.570
to train a base module or a
more complex advanced model.

00:19:53.570 --> 00:19:55.230
I'll get into that
a bit later on.

00:19:55.230 --> 00:19:57.140
So once I've trained
my model, I'll

00:19:57.140 --> 00:19:59.000
get an email when it completes.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:01.010
And my next step
is to go in here

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:03.710
and see how my model performed.

00:20:03.710 --> 00:20:06.740
And there's a bunch of different
machine learning metrics here.

00:20:06.740 --> 00:20:08.210
What I want to
focus on is what's

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:09.680
called a confusion matrix.

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:11.159
It looks confusing.

00:20:11.159 --> 00:20:12.450
It's called a confusing matrix.

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:14.430
But it's actually
not that confusing.

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:15.510
Let's take a look.

00:20:15.510 --> 00:20:17.150
So in ideal confusion
matrix, we want

00:20:17.150 --> 00:20:20.000
to see a strong diagonal
down the top left, which

00:20:20.000 --> 00:20:21.470
is what we get here.

00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:24.410
What this is telling
us is when we uploaded

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:27.230
our images to AutoML, it
took a portion of them

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:30.710
to train the model, but then
it set aside a small percentage

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:33.710
of those images to see how
the model did on data that it

00:20:33.710 --> 00:20:35.259
didn't see during training.

00:20:35.259 --> 00:20:37.550
So this is how the model
performed on that smaller test

00:20:37.550 --> 00:20:38.870
set of images.

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:41.390
So what this is telling us
is that for all of my images

00:20:41.390 --> 00:20:44.030
that were actually
altocumulus clouds,

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:48.620
my model predicted 76%
of those correctly.

00:20:48.620 --> 00:20:52.030
And we can see the percentages
we get all down here

00:20:52.030 --> 00:20:54.650
Now for this model, I
actually trained both the base

00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:56.370
and the advanced version.

00:20:56.370 --> 00:20:59.300
And AutoML has a great way
to compare different versions

00:20:59.300 --> 00:21:00.495
of your model.

00:21:00.495 --> 00:21:02.870
So let's say even if you just
trained a bunch of versions

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:04.828
of the base module, let's
say you train it once

00:21:04.828 --> 00:21:06.140
with 500 images.

00:21:06.140 --> 00:21:08.750
Then you add 500 more images
and retrain your model,

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:10.550
and you want to see
how it performs.

00:21:10.550 --> 00:21:13.340
So I can compare them here.

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:16.340
And now I can see how
it did on the base

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:18.375
compared to the advanced model.

00:21:18.375 --> 00:21:19.500
And this looks pretty good.

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:23.210
It looks like all of these
went up significantly

00:21:23.210 --> 00:21:26.270
when I went from the base
to the advanced model.

00:21:26.270 --> 00:21:29.154
But hey, wait, this one
actually went down 12%.

00:21:29.154 --> 00:21:31.070
And wouldn't you expect
the advanced knowledge

00:21:31.070 --> 00:21:34.040
to perform better
across the board?

00:21:34.040 --> 00:21:36.110
Well, what this
actually points out

00:21:36.110 --> 00:21:38.880
is that there may be some
problems with my training data.

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:41.690
So if we look at
where it got confused,

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:45.080
it looks like it was labeling
a lot of my altostratus images

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:46.690
as cumulus clouds.

00:21:46.690 --> 00:21:49.160
And I can actually
click on this,

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:52.690
and I can see the images that
my model was confused on.

00:21:52.690 --> 00:21:54.440
So it turns out that
these images actually

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:55.940
are a little
confusing, and they may

00:21:55.940 --> 00:21:57.950
have been labeled incorrectly.

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:01.680
And the advanced model was able
to identify these shortcomings

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:03.570
in my training data for me.

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:04.490
So now I can go in.

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:06.770
Next time I update
my model, I can

00:22:06.770 --> 00:22:12.135
make sure to add better
images of altocumulus clouds.

00:22:12.135 --> 00:22:14.260
So the next step-- and the
most fun in my opinion--

00:22:14.260 --> 00:22:16.780
is generating predictions
on our trained model.

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:19.370
And there's a couple of
different ways to do this.

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:21.610
One is I can use the web UI.

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:23.560
So that's what I'm
going to do right now.

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:25.300
I can upload an
image here, and I

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:29.020
can see what AutoML Vision
is going to predict.

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:34.390
So this was indeed a
picture of a cirrus cloud,

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:36.430
and this is pretty awesome.

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:41.050
Our model is 99% confident
that this is a cirrus cloud.

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:43.600
So we can use the UI to
generate predictions.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:46.090
This is a good quick and
easy way to check your model,

00:22:46.090 --> 00:22:48.706
see how it's performing right
after it's been trained.

00:22:48.706 --> 00:22:50.080
But chances are
you actually want

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:52.450
to build an application
that's going

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:53.650
to query your trained model.

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:56.660
And there's a couple of
different ways to do this.

00:22:56.660 --> 00:22:58.550
I want to highlight
one in particular,

00:22:58.550 --> 00:23:01.190
which is the Vision API.

00:23:01.190 --> 00:23:03.800
So you saw briefly
in the past slides

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:06.340
what a request to the
Vision API looks like.

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:07.960
And what I want you
to focus on here

00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:10.120
is that the request
really doesn't look that

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:12.230
much different with AutoML.

00:23:12.230 --> 00:23:14.230
So the only things we
need to add to our request

00:23:14.230 --> 00:23:16.960
is this custom label
detection parameter.

00:23:16.960 --> 00:23:19.180
And then once we
train our model,

00:23:19.180 --> 00:23:21.930
we get access to this ID
for our trained model,

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:23.890
and so we need to add
that to our request.

00:23:23.890 --> 00:23:26.200
Only I have access
to this model version

00:23:26.200 --> 00:23:28.699
or whoever else I share
this project with.

00:23:28.699 --> 00:23:30.490
So let's say that I'm
using the Vision API.

00:23:30.490 --> 00:23:32.290
Let's say I had
some weather app.

00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:35.109
And first, I wanted
my app only to detect

00:23:35.109 --> 00:23:36.400
is there a cloud in this image?

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:38.410
I was using the
Vision API, but then I

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:40.790
decided that wasn't
actually good enough.

00:23:40.790 --> 00:23:42.520
I need to detect
the types of clouds

00:23:42.520 --> 00:23:44.140
that I'm seeing in these images.

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.090
I don't really need to
change much about how

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:47.326
my app is architected.

00:23:47.326 --> 00:23:48.700
In order for that
to work, I just

00:23:48.700 --> 00:23:51.640
need to switch out or add
this custom label detection

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:55.270
parameter, along with the
model that I've just trained.

00:23:55.270 --> 00:23:57.790
So that's how we generate
predictions on our model.

00:23:57.790 --> 00:24:00.130
To show you how easy it
is to build an app that

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:04.130
uses our trained model, I built
a super simple web app here,

00:24:04.130 --> 00:24:06.790
where I can upload
a photo of a cloud.

00:24:06.790 --> 00:24:08.200
And we'll use this one.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.100
And it's going to call my model.

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:12.970
It's going to tell me a little
bit more about that cloud.

00:24:12.970 --> 00:24:14.950
So this is a cumulonimbus cloud.

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:16.342
And if you see
this type of cloud

00:24:16.342 --> 00:24:18.550
while you're on a plane,
probably not the best sign--

00:24:18.550 --> 00:24:20.540
might be some turbulence.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:22.492
So this is just a
simple web app that

00:24:22.492 --> 00:24:24.700
queries that model using
the Vision API, which I just

00:24:24.700 --> 00:24:27.850
showed you, and it gives us a
little bit more data on what

00:24:27.850 --> 00:24:30.800
these types of
clouds actually mean.

00:24:30.800 --> 00:24:33.500
So that is AutoML Vision.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:36.340
I'm going to go
back to the slides.

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:39.040
And I wanted to highlight
some companies that are using

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:41.350
AutoML Vision in production.

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:42.880
The first is Disney.

00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:46.000
Disney built a model trained
to identify specific Disney

00:24:46.000 --> 00:24:49.074
characters, products,
categories, and dominant colors

00:24:49.074 --> 00:24:51.615
that were specific to the images
that they have in their data

00:24:51.615 --> 00:24:52.540
set.

00:24:52.540 --> 00:24:53.920
And they've
integrated this model

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:56.210
into Disney's search engine.

00:24:56.210 --> 00:24:59.020
And it's helping their users
get more relevant results

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:01.510
and find their products faster.

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:03.940
Urban Outfitters is
a clothing company,

00:25:03.940 --> 00:25:05.830
and they have a similar
use case to Disney.

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:07.240
They trained a
model to recognize

00:25:07.240 --> 00:25:11.680
specific types of clothing
patterns and neckline styles.

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:14.560
And they're using that to build
a comprehensive set of product

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:15.580
attributes.

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:17.650
So through this,
they're able to improve

00:25:17.650 --> 00:25:22.030
product recommendations, search
results, and product filters.

00:25:22.030 --> 00:25:26.190
The last example is the
Zoological Society of London.

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:30.520
The Zoological Society of London
has cameras deployed all over

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:34.690
in the wild to identify
different types of wildlife

00:25:34.690 --> 00:25:36.220
in their images.

00:25:36.220 --> 00:25:39.040
And rather than having someone
manually review all the footage

00:25:39.040 --> 00:25:42.070
that they're seeing, they
built an AutoML model

00:25:42.070 --> 00:25:45.010
to identify all the
different types of wildlife

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:46.705
that they're seeing
in those images.

00:25:49.624 --> 00:25:51.540
So I definitely encourage
you to go learn more

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:53.100
about AutoML Vision.

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:56.460
You can go to
cloud.google.com/automl.

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:59.010
And there you can fill out a
form to sign up for the alpha.

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:01.470
We're really interested to
hear what types of use cases

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:03.120
you have for AutoML.

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:04.770
There's more details
in the blog posts

00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:06.930
where we announced AutoML.

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:09.870
There's also an intro
video, a podcast

00:26:09.870 --> 00:26:12.150
that goes into a little
more detail about how

00:26:12.150 --> 00:26:13.732
this works under the hood.

00:26:13.732 --> 00:26:15.690
And then if you want to
try out the Vision API,

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:18.600
if you want to use the demo
that Elias showed earlier,

00:26:18.600 --> 00:26:22.290
you can head over to
cloud.google.com/vision to try

00:26:22.290 --> 00:26:23.850
that out with your own images.

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:25.620
I definitely
recommend doing that.

00:26:25.620 --> 00:26:27.245
Let's say you want
to see if the Vision

00:26:27.245 --> 00:26:29.056
API is right for your
app, but you don't

00:26:29.056 --> 00:26:30.180
want to write any code yet.

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:32.220
So you can just upload
your images there,

00:26:32.220 --> 00:26:34.230
see the JSON response
you'll get back,

00:26:34.230 --> 00:26:36.270
and see if the
Vision API is going

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:40.950
to be a good fit for
your application.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:45.612
So thank you, and I'm going
to bring Elias back up here.

00:26:45.612 --> 00:26:47.820
ELIAS PINTO: So we're going
to be going into Q&amp;A here

00:26:47.820 --> 00:26:48.610
in just a moment.

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:50.418
So we we'll be back in a minute.

00:26:50.418 --> 00:26:51.459
SARA ROBINSON: Thank you.

00:29:31.149 --> 00:29:32.690
ELIAS PINTO: Welcome
back, everybody.

00:29:32.690 --> 00:29:35.150
Hopefully, you've been busy
putting some questions there.

00:29:35.150 --> 00:29:37.220
And we've bubbled up a
couple of questions here

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:38.480
to get us started.

00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:40.910
So do you want to take
the first one here?

00:29:40.910 --> 00:29:42.290
I'll read it first.

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:44.880
So "how do I sign
up for AutoML?"

00:29:44.880 --> 00:29:46.580
SARA ROBINSON: That
is a great question.

00:29:46.580 --> 00:29:50.660
To sign for AutoML, go to
cloud.google.com/automl.

00:29:50.660 --> 00:29:52.784
And there you'll see a
link to fill out a form.

00:29:52.784 --> 00:29:54.200
And this is where
we're collecting

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:57.320
details about the use case
that you want to use AutoML.

00:29:57.320 --> 00:30:01.130
So we'll review that, and
then you should hear back

00:30:01.130 --> 00:30:03.950
within a couple of weeks or so.

00:30:03.950 --> 00:30:05.222
Let's see the next question.

00:30:05.222 --> 00:30:06.430
So you want to take this one?

00:30:06.430 --> 00:30:07.190
ELIAS PINTO: Sure.

00:30:07.190 --> 00:30:10.010
SARA ROBINSON: "What industries
do you see using AutoML?"

00:30:10.010 --> 00:30:11.760
ELIAS PINTO: The cool
thing is that we are

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:13.170
democratizing the technology.

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:19.130
So really, any business
that deals with image data

00:30:19.130 --> 00:30:21.680
and which arguably
is the vast majority,

00:30:21.680 --> 00:30:26.190
whether it is actual
image data that has--

00:30:26.190 --> 00:30:29.120
and specifically, I'm talking
about AutoML Vision here.

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:34.740
If you have to, as part of
your business, identify things.

00:30:34.740 --> 00:30:37.250
And we talked about
Disney, about identifying

00:30:37.250 --> 00:30:39.980
their characters, and
that intellectual property

00:30:39.980 --> 00:30:42.660
across a very large data set.

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:48.980
We're able to really apply the
mature machine learning models

00:30:48.980 --> 00:30:54.800
here to a lot of verticals,
like manufacturing, retail,

00:30:54.800 --> 00:30:59.430
any sort of a
customer-facing applications.

00:30:59.430 --> 00:31:02.130
There are way too many
industries here to--

00:31:02.130 --> 00:31:04.700
I don't to limit ourselves
because even if you're just

00:31:04.700 --> 00:31:10.640
processing resumes
or business cards,

00:31:10.640 --> 00:31:16.430
we can do a lot to expedite
what your business does,

00:31:16.430 --> 00:31:20.300
lower the cost of entry
because, again, it's an API.

00:31:20.300 --> 00:31:23.120
You don't have to build
another Google data center.

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:24.090
We've done it.

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:26.990
And so you're able to just
leverage that and just

00:31:26.990 --> 00:31:29.120
pay for what you use.

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:31.800
OK, let me read the next one.

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.460
So "how do I know
whether I should

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:38.570
use AutoML or build an
image classification

00:31:38.570 --> 00:31:40.061
model from scratch?"

00:31:40.061 --> 00:31:41.060
You want to do that one?

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:42.851
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah
that's a good question.

00:31:42.851 --> 00:31:45.620
So what AutoML excels at
is image classification.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:48.710
So you give it a lot
of labeled images.

00:31:48.710 --> 00:31:51.837
It's able to output a label
or a tag for that image.

00:31:51.837 --> 00:31:54.170
But let's say you want to do
something a little bit more

00:31:54.170 --> 00:31:57.500
complex, like you want to
identify bounding boxes

00:31:57.500 --> 00:31:58.160
in your image.

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:02.030
So you want to say this
is a specific product

00:32:02.030 --> 00:32:03.540
that our company
builds, and we want

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:05.130
to identify where
we're seeing it

00:32:05.130 --> 00:32:07.382
in image with the bounding box.

00:32:07.382 --> 00:32:09.590
You would need to build a
custom image classification

00:32:09.590 --> 00:32:12.881
model with TensorFlow or
Cloud Machine Learning Engine

00:32:12.881 --> 00:32:13.380
to do that.

00:32:13.380 --> 00:32:17.090
Or let's say you wanted to
identify regions in an image.

00:32:17.090 --> 00:32:20.000
That's called
masking-- so labeling

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.760
specific regions in
whatever types of images

00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:24.080
you're labeling.

00:32:24.080 --> 00:32:27.610
That's another use case for
a custom image classification

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:29.060
model.

00:32:29.060 --> 00:32:31.470
But if you would like
to do image tagging--

00:32:31.470 --> 00:32:34.210
so outputs and labels
based on image--

00:32:34.210 --> 00:32:36.050
AutoML is great
across the board.

00:32:36.050 --> 00:32:37.970
And the really cool
thing is it doesn't

00:32:37.970 --> 00:32:42.552
require you to have to write
any of that model code yourself.

00:32:42.552 --> 00:32:43.645
ELIAS PINTO: Awesome.

00:32:43.645 --> 00:32:46.500
Let me read you the last
one, the next one here.

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:49.990
"How long does training
a model really take?

00:32:49.990 --> 00:32:53.270
And are there any best
practices to consider in order

00:32:53.270 --> 00:32:57.560
to speed up this process
or improve accuracy?"

00:32:57.560 --> 00:33:00.320
Sounds like somebody really
wants to train their model.

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:02.210
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah,
that's a good question.

00:33:02.210 --> 00:33:04.190
I can take that one.

00:33:04.190 --> 00:33:06.050
So how long does
training model take?

00:33:06.050 --> 00:33:10.940
For the base model, I've found
it takes about an hour, maybe

00:33:10.940 --> 00:33:12.650
a little bit less.

00:33:12.650 --> 00:33:15.500
The advanced model
for my cloud data set,

00:33:15.500 --> 00:33:20.570
which had about 1,500 images,
took just about two to three

00:33:20.570 --> 00:33:21.637
hours to train.

00:33:21.637 --> 00:33:23.720
Although as you saw in the
UI, the advanced model,

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:27.320
depending on how many images you
have, can take up to four hours

00:33:27.320 --> 00:33:31.020
to train because it's a much
more complex type of model.

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:33.710
So it really depends
on how many images

00:33:33.710 --> 00:33:37.430
are in your data set and the
complexity of and the variety

00:33:37.430 --> 00:33:38.930
of those images.

00:33:38.930 --> 00:33:41.600
Some tips to improve accuracy.

00:33:41.600 --> 00:33:44.990
As I showed briefly, looking at
the Evaluate tab in the AutoML

00:33:44.990 --> 00:33:46.970
UI is super useful.

00:33:46.970 --> 00:33:48.540
I showed the confusion matrix.

00:33:48.540 --> 00:33:50.840
There are a couple
other metrics there.

00:33:50.840 --> 00:33:53.060
But what I love about that
confusion matrix in the UI

00:33:53.060 --> 00:33:55.520
is that you can
actually click and see

00:33:55.520 --> 00:33:57.470
what's being labeled
correctly, what's

00:33:57.470 --> 00:33:58.770
being labeled incorrectly.

00:33:58.770 --> 00:34:00.980
So I think that's the best
way to improve accuracy

00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:03.020
on your model because
you want to remember

00:34:03.020 --> 00:34:04.670
that your model's
only going to be

00:34:04.670 --> 00:34:08.420
as good as the quality of
training data that you give it.

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:11.513
So that Evaluate tab can
help you identify problems

00:34:11.513 --> 00:34:12.429
in your training data.

00:34:12.429 --> 00:34:14.637
So then you can go back,
look at the matrix, and see,

00:34:14.637 --> 00:34:17.120
OK, maybe a lot of my
data was mislabeled,

00:34:17.120 --> 00:34:20.360
or maybe there just wasn't
enough of this particular label

00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:21.889
category in my data set.

00:34:21.889 --> 00:34:23.870
And you can go back
and improve it.

00:34:26.142 --> 00:34:27.600
ELIAS PINTO: You
read the next one.

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:28.920
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, let's
take a look at the next one.

00:34:28.920 --> 00:34:32.030
"Once a model is built, say for
identifying a particular brand

00:34:32.030 --> 00:34:33.889
of toy, who owns that model--

00:34:33.889 --> 00:34:35.520
Google or the customer
who built it?"

00:34:35.520 --> 00:34:37.520
ELIAS PINTO: This is a
really important question

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:39.679
to get answered.

00:34:39.679 --> 00:34:41.576
You own your data.

00:34:41.576 --> 00:34:42.659
That's an important point.

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:43.409
I'll say it again.

00:34:43.409 --> 00:34:44.520
You own your data.

00:34:44.520 --> 00:34:47.710
Google does not own the model.

00:34:47.710 --> 00:34:50.250
Very important to mention that.

00:34:50.250 --> 00:34:54.179
We do not use your data
to retrain our models.

00:34:54.179 --> 00:34:58.380
For example, Sara showed
the cloud classification.

00:34:58.380 --> 00:35:00.870
That the cloud
training, and the data,

00:35:00.870 --> 00:35:06.450
and the pictures themselves
remain under Sara's ownership.

00:35:06.450 --> 00:35:11.000
And there is a very
hard break here

00:35:11.000 --> 00:35:14.100
that we don't use that data
or information to train

00:35:14.100 --> 00:35:15.180
anything outside of it.

00:35:15.180 --> 00:35:18.930
So folks that are worried
about intellectual property,

00:35:18.930 --> 00:35:21.780
or how should I train my model?

00:35:21.780 --> 00:35:27.660
Should I use my special sauce
with my special pictures?

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:29.700
There is no danger
of that getting out.

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:33.270
And we don't use that data to
retrain anything else outside

00:35:33.270 --> 00:35:35.620
of your own models.

00:35:35.620 --> 00:35:38.650
OK, I'll read the next one.

00:35:38.650 --> 00:35:40.650
We're on the last
one on this slide.

00:35:40.650 --> 00:35:44.180
"So I want to classify
on a type of image

00:35:44.180 --> 00:35:48.770
or a brand, which isn't
covered by the Vision API.

00:35:48.770 --> 00:35:52.370
What can I use to extend the
classification that is already

00:35:52.370 --> 00:35:55.682
available through
AutoML/Vision API?"

00:35:55.682 --> 00:35:57.390
I think you touched
on that a little bit.

00:35:57.390 --> 00:35:57.890
You want to go back
to it a little bit?

00:35:57.890 --> 00:35:58.940
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, yeah.

00:35:58.940 --> 00:36:00.080
I touched on that a
little bit earlier,

00:36:00.080 --> 00:36:01.490
but I'll go back into it.

00:36:01.490 --> 00:36:03.279
So I mentioned with
the cloud example--

00:36:03.279 --> 00:36:04.820
so let's say that
I just want to know

00:36:04.820 --> 00:36:06.440
is there a cloud in my image?

00:36:06.440 --> 00:36:07.700
Vision API is great for that.

00:36:07.700 --> 00:36:10.369
But then I wanted to go one
step further and say, OK,

00:36:10.369 --> 00:36:11.660
what types of clouds are these?

